Full Grok response: {
  "topic": "The signal() Function",
  "explanation": "The signal() function in C is used to set a handler for a specific signal, allowing programs to respond to asynchronous events like interrupts. It is essential for handling signals such as SIGINT (generated by Ctrl+C), enabling graceful error handling, cleanup, or custom responses instead of abrupt termination.",
  "syntax": "void (*signal(int signum, void (*func)(int)))(int)",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "#include <signal.h>",
    "#include <unistd.h>",
    "",
    "static void sig_int(int signo) {",
    "    printf(\"Caught signal %d\\n\", signo);",
    "}",
    "",
    "int main() {",
    "    if (signal(SIGINT, sig_int) == SIG_ERR) {",
    "        printf(\"Error setting signal handler\\n\");",
    "        return 1;",
    "    }",
    "    printf(\"Running for 5 seconds... Press Ctrl+C to trigger SIGINT\\n\");",
    "    sleep(5);",
    "    printf(\"Program continuing after sleep\\n\");",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This program demonstrates the signal() function by registering sig_int as the handler for SIGINT. When the program runs and receives a SIGINT signal (e.g., via Ctrl+C during the sleep period), the sig_int function is called to handle it, printing the signal number and showing how the handler intercepts the signal before the program potentially terminates."
}
