  "topic": "Return Value of system() with Signal Termination",
  "explanation": "The return value of the system() function in C represents the termination status of the shell that executed the command. When a command is terminated by a signal, the shell's termination status is typically 128 plus the signal number, allowing programs to detect and handle signal-based interruptions. This is useful for error handling and understanding command failures due to signals like SIGINT or SIGQUIT.",
  "syntax": "int system(const char *command)",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "#include <stdlib.h>",
    "",
    "int main() {",
    "    int status = system(\"sleep 30\");",
    "    printf(\"Exit status: %d\\n\", status);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This program calls system() to execute 'sleep 30', which pauses for 30 seconds. If you interrupt it with Ctrl+C (SIGINT), the program prints an exit status of 130 (128 + 2, where 2 is SIGINT). If interrupted with Ctrl+\\ (SIGQUIT), it prints 131 (128 + 3). This demonstrates how system() returns the shell's termination status adjusted for signals, showing signal detection in practice."
}
