Full Grok response: {
  "topic": "Thread Creation with pthread_create",
  "explanation": "pthread_create is a function in the POSIX threads library that creates a new thread of execution within a process. It is used to enable concurrency, allowing multiple threads to run simultaneously, which can improve program efficiency for tasks like parallel processing or handling multiple operations at once.",
  "syntax": "int pthread_create(pthread_t *thread, const pthread_attr_t *attr, void *(*start_routine)(void *), void *arg)",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "#include <pthread.h>",
    "#include <unistd.h>",
    "",
    "void *thread_function(void *arg) {",
    "    printf(\"New thread: Process ID: %d, Thread ID: %lu\\n\", getpid(), pthread_self());",
    "    return NULL;",
    "}",
    "",
    "int main() {",
    "    pthread_t thread;",
    "    pthread_create(&thread, NULL, thread_function, NULL);",
    "    printf(\"Main thread: Process ID: %d, Thread ID: %lu\\n\", getpid(), pthread_self());",
    "    pthread_join(thread, NULL);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This program demonstrates thread creation by defining a thread function that prints the process and thread IDs of the new thread. In the main function, pthread_create is used to start the new thread, which executes thread_function. The main thread then prints its own process and thread IDs and waits for the new thread to finish with pthread_join, showing how a new thread is created and runs concurrently with the main thread."
}
