  "explanation": "The restrict keyword is a type qualifier in C applied to pointers. It informs the compiler that the pointer is the only way to access the object it points to for the duration of its scope. This helps the compiler perform optimizations, such as assuming no aliasing, which can improve performance by reducing unnecessary memory checks.",
  "syntax": "void function(type *restrict ptr);",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "",
    "void addOne(int *restrict ptr) {",
    "    *ptr += 1;  // Modify the value through the restrict pointer",
    "}",
    "",
    "int main() {",
    "    int x = 5;  // Variable to be modified",
    "    addOne(&x);  // Pass address to function",
    "    printf(\"%d\\n\", x);  // Output the modified value",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This program defines a function addOne that uses a restrict pointer to increment an integer value. In main, we initialize x to 5, pass its address to addOne, and then print the result. The restrict keyword demonstrates its use by indicating to the compiler that ptr is the only access path to the object, potentially allowing optimizations, while the program shows the pointer successfully modifying the value."
}
