  "topic": "pthread_mutex_lock",
  "explanation": "pthread_mutex_lock is a function in the POSIX threads library that locks a mutex object, ensuring that only one thread can access a protected section of code at a time. It is used to prevent race conditions, such as when multiple threads read from or write to the same shared resource like a file descriptor, which could lead to data corruption or inconsistent results if accessed concurrently.",
  "syntax": "int pthread_mutex_lock(pthread_mutex_t *mutex);",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "#include <pthread.h>",
    "#include <fcntl.h>",
    "#include <unistd.h>",
    "pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;",
    "int fd;",
    "void *thread_function(void *arg) {",
    "    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);",
    "    write(fd, \"Hello from thread\\n\", 16);",
    "    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);",
    "    return NULL;",
    "}",
    "int main() {",
    "    fd = open(\"test.txt\", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT, 0644);",
    "    if (fd == -1) { return 1; }",
    "    pthread_t thread1, thread2;",
    "    pthread_create(&thread1, NULL, thread_function, NULL);",
    "    pthread_create(&thread2, NULL, thread_function, NULL);",
    "    pthread_join(thread1, NULL);",
    "    pthread_join(thread2, NULL);",
    "    close(fd);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This program opens a file and creates two threads that write to the same file descriptor. The pthread_mutex_lock function locks the mutex before writing, ensuring only one thread accesses the file at a time, which prevents race conditions and demonstrates how mutexes protect shared resources like file descriptors."
}
